\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers, listed in Table~\ref{tab:models}. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API;\footnote{All API models were accessed via OpenRouter between March--April 2026. Model snapshots may have changed after this period.} six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with Activation-aware Weight Quantization (AWQ) INT4 on an NVIDIA RTX~6000 Ada (48\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following the provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy on the pairwise judgment task (Table~\ref{tab:models}). We study three panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (all odd, majority-vote threshold $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$). Exhaustive enumeration yields $\binom{15}{3} = 455$ panels at $K{=}3$, $\binom{15}{5} = 3{,}003$ at $K{=}5$, and $\binom{15}{7} = 6{,}435$ at $K{=}7$, covering every combination of families and tiers. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor. The primary analysis uses $K{=}3$; \S\ref{sec:cross_k} extends to larger panels.

\subsection{Benchmarks and Attack Types}

Two benchmarks test distinct evaluation capabilities: MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, a knowledge-intensive multiple-choice benchmark spanning 57 subjects, and ARC \citep{clark2018arc}, a reasoning-intensive science question benchmark. We sample 300 items from each. All models use temperature~0 for deterministic outputs (temperature has negligible effect on $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$; Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}).

Each benchmark is paired with six attack types: (1)~\textbf{prompt injection}, adversarial instructions overriding the evaluation prompt \citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023not}; (2)~\textbf{sycophancy}, flattering language exploiting judge preferences \citep{wang2023fair}; (3)~\textbf{score manipulation}, explicit numerical cues anchoring scoring; (4)~\textbf{verbosity bias}, padding with irrelevant detail \citep{zheng2023judging}; (5)~\textbf{position bias}, reordering to exploit positional preferences \citep{wang2023fair}; and (6)~\textbf{authority bias}, fabricated credentials inflating credibility. The 2 benchmarks $\times$ 6 attacks produce 12 conditions, each evaluated across all 455 panels (details in Appendix~\ref{app:attack_details}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Bench.} & \textbf{Attack} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & \textbf{Dom.} \\
\midrule
MMLU & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Score manip.   & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
MMLU & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Position bias  & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
\addlinespace
ARC  & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Score manip.   & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Position bias  & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
ARC  & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Standardized regression coefficients for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across 12 conditions (455 panels, log-log OLS). $+^{*}$/$-^{*}$: FDR-significant positive/negative (Benjamini--Hochberg, $q < 0.05$); n.s.: not significant. Dom.\ column: $\eta$ = $\eta_{\max}$ sole dominant, $K$ = $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sole dominant, Co = co-dominant \citep{budescu1993dominance}.}
\label{tab:dominance}
\end{table}

\subsection{Vulnerability Metrics}

Per-judge vulnerability under attack $a$ is the fraction of items where judge $j$ is fooled: $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a {=} 1)$, estimated empirically over the item set. The weakest-link vulnerability $\eta_{\max}(a) = \max_{j \in \mathcal{P}} \eta_j(a)$ captures the most susceptible member of each panel. The panel attack success rate $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ is the fraction of items where at least $t$ judges are simultaneously fooled. Panel diversity is quantified by $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (Equation~\ref{eq:keff}), computed from pairwise MI between judges' three-category winner labels (model~A wins, model~B wins, tie) on clean data, using the plug-in estimator with Miller--Madow bias correction. The $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI}(i,j))$ term measures pairwise alienation: 1 when independent, 0 under full dependence. Under a Gaussian copula this yields $K_{\mathrm{eff}} = K - \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i}\sum_{j \neq i} \rho_{ij}^2$ \citep{nelsen2006copulas}; goodness-of-fit rejects the Gaussian copula for 97--100\% of pairs, yet plug-in MI and closed-form rankings agree closely ($\rho{\approx}0.86$; Appendix~\ref{app:copula_gof}). Together, $\eta_{\max}$ (weakest-link channel) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity channel) serve as the two predictors in the regression framework below.

\subsection{Aggregation Methods}

Beyond majority vote ($\geq \lceil K/2 \rceil$ approvals), we evaluate three alternatives: \emph{accuracy-weighted vote} ($w_j \propto \mathrm{acc}_j$; accept if $\sum_j w_j \cdot \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] > 0.5$), \emph{threshold vote} (unanimity, $t{=}K$), and \emph{Bayesian calibration} ($P(\text{correct} \mid \mathbf{v}) \propto P(\text{correct}) \prod_j P(v_j \mid \text{correct})$; accept if posterior $> 0.5$).

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

To disentangle the contributions of panel diversity and individual judge robustness, we fit a log-log OLS regression for each of the 12 conditions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:regression}
\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon) = \alpha + \beta_1 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}}) + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max} + \epsilon),
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ to handle zero values (sensitivity to $\epsilon$ is analyzed in Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon}). Standardized coefficients $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = \hat{\beta} \cdot \mathrm{SD}(\log x) / \mathrm{SD}(\log y)$ enable direct comparison of effect magnitudes across predictors and conditions. We apply Benjamini--Hochberg (BH) correction for multiple testing, controlling the false discovery rate (FDR) per predictor across the 12 conditions.

Dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance} decomposes $R^2$ into each predictor's average incremental contribution across all subsets. Generalized additive models (GAMs) with smooth terms test whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect direction varies between targeted attacks (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias) and shared-bias attacks (sycophancy, verbosity, authority bias).

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness_method}

The 455 panels share judges combinatorially (each appears in 91 panels), inducing correlated residuals. We apply three corrections \citep[see][for a practitioner's guide]{cameron2015practitioners}: clustered standard errors (SE; CR1, 3-position max-SE), mixed-effects models (random judge intercepts), and wild cluster bootstrap ($B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, 3-position max-$p$) following \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}. Cluster counts (13, ${\leq}11$, ${\leq}9$ at $K{=}3,5,7$) fall below the ${\geq}20$ threshold; we use Rademacher weights following \citet{mackinnon2018wild}. Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered SE serves as an $\epsilon$-free robustness check (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_methods}).

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Weakest-Link Dominance at \texorpdfstring{$K{=}3$}{K=3}}
\label{sec:dominance}

Table~\ref{tab:dominance} reports the log-log OLS regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) for all 12 conditions across the 455 panels ($K{=}3$, 15 judges from Table~\ref{tab:models}).

Under OLS, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is positive and FDR-significant in all 12/12 conditions. $\eta_{\max}$ is significant in 10/12 (exceptions: MMLU $\times$ score manipulation and ARC $\times$ position bias), consistent with Theorem~\ref{thm:monotonicity}. Dominance analysis yields 10 co-dominant, 2 $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-dominant, and zero $\eta_{\max}$-dominant conditions. However, the $r = 0.70$ $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--$\eta_{\max}$ correlation raises confound concerns, and OLS ignores the non-independence of the 455 panels (Appendix~\ref{app:keff_dist}).

Each judge appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ of the 455 panels, and each pair co-occurs in 13. This shared structure causes OLS to underestimate standard errors by approximately $2.5{\times}$. Figure~\ref{fig:robustness} compares OLS against three corrections: both predictors attenuate under bootstrap ($\eta_{\max}$ 3/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 4/12), with clustered SE widest ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 0/12; Appendix~\ref{app:forest_plot}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} \\
\midrule
OLS                    & 10/12 & 12/12 \\
Clustered SE           & 4/12  & 0/12  \\
Mixed effects          & 6/12  & 3/12  \\
Wild bootstrap         & 3/12  & 4/12  \\
Frac.\ logit (clust.)  & 6/12  & 9/12  \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Inference method sensitivity at $K{=}3$ (455 panels, 12 conditions). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance ranges from 0/12 (clustered SE) to 12/12 (OLS); all methods preserve positive direction (12/12).}
\label{tab:method_comparison}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_robustness_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Significance counts (FDR $< 0.05$) for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across four correction methods over 12 experimental conditions ($K{=}3$). Wild cluster bootstrap and clustered standard errors are recommended for the 15-judge combinatorial design.}
\label{fig:robustness}
\end{figure}

Power analysis yields a minimum detectable effect (MDE) of $\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.14$ at 80\% power (decreasing to 0.048 at $K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:power_analysis}). The bootstrap attenuation at $K{=}3$ (OLS 12/12 $\to$ bootstrap 4/12) is consistent with this MDE, though positive coefficient direction (12/12) is preserved across all methods (Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}).

\subsection{Panel-Size-Dependent Predictor Shift}
\label{sec:cross_k}

Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison} reveals that the $K{=}3$ pattern is method-sensitive: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance ranges from 0/12 (clustered SE) to 12/12 (OLS), with fractional logit giving 9/12. This contrasts with $K{\geq}5$, where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s dominance is consistent across methods (bootstrap 7/12 at $K{=}5$; fractional logit 9/12).

We repeat the wild cluster bootstrap at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$, constructing all $\binom{15}{K}$ panels with $K$-position max-$p$ correction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_regime_transition.pdf}
\caption{Predictor shift across panel sizes. $\eta_{\max}$ significance (orange) declines from 3/12 to 2/12, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance (blue) rises from 4/12 to 7/12 as $K$ increases from 3 to 5. Shaded region marks the predictor shift zone $K^* \in (3, 5)$. Bootstrap: $B{=}9999$, Rademacher weights, position-permutation max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$.}
\label{fig:regime_transition}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dominance_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Per-condition predictor dominance across panel sizes (wild cluster bootstrap, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$). At $K{=}3$, dominance is heterogeneous (co-dominant, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-only, $\eta_{\max}$-only, and non-significant conditions coexist); at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominates uniformly while $\eta_{\max}$ disappears.}
\label{fig:dominance_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:regime_transition} reveals a predictor shift between $K{=}3$ and $K{=}5$, with per-condition detail in Figure~\ref{fig:dominance_heatmap}. At $K{=}3$, both predictors contribute modestly ($\eta_{\max}$ 3/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 4/12), with co-determination in two conditions. At $K{=}5$, $\eta_{\max}$ drops to 2/12 while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ rises to 7/12, all positive. Standardized coefficient magnitudes corroborate: $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ for $\eta_{\max}$ declines monotonically ($0.266 \to 0.101 \to 0.084$) while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s trajectory peaks at $K{=}5$ ($0.349 \to 0.437 \to 0.401$), providing a power-independent characterization of the predictor shift that does not depend on sample size or cluster count. Restricting to the four $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-stable attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}) confirms that the signal is not driven by measurement-unstable conditions.

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
\label{sec:rf_contrast}

We simulate non-adversarial noise by randomly flipping judge votes at observed per-judge error rates. Random faults yield $R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$ ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant 2/2); adaptive attack reduces predictability ($R^2{=}0.275$ at $K{=}5$) but $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains power (7/12). The predictability gap narrows from ${\sim}0.6$ ($K{=}3$) to $0.43$ ($K{=}5$).

\subsection{Positive Direction Across Attack Types}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM bootstrap confirms positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction in all 12 conditions (88.9--100\% positive rate; Figure~\ref{fig:gam_split}), with no magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.200$, $0.423$).

\subsection{Aggregation Rules and the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 11+/0$-$  & 0.829 \\
Majority        & 10/12 & 12/12 & 12+/0$-$  & 0.327 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 12/12 & 12+/0$-$  & 0.328 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 6/12  & 5+/1$-$   & 0.087 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison ($K{=}3$, 15 models, 455 panels, OLS log-log). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Table~\ref{tab:dominance} uses per-condition FDR; this table uses pooled FDR across conditions.}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}


Predictability ranges from threshold ($R^2{=}0.829$) to Bayesian ($R^2{=}0.087$); Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12), yielding partial defense (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_details}).

\paragraph{Fractional Logit Robustness.}
Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered SE shows consistent positive association: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance is 9/12 across all panel sizes ($K{=}3,5,7$), with 98.6\% directional concordance with OLS (71/72; Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}).

\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.93\textwidth]{figures/fig_framework.pdf}
\caption{The shifting bottleneck in LLM judge panels. \textbf{(a)}~At $K{=}3$, one compromised judge flips the majority---$\eta_{\max}$ dominates. \textbf{(b)}~A predictor shift emerges at $K^* \in (3,5)$. \textbf{(c)}~At $K{\geq}5$, the adversary must compromise multiple independent judges---$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the dominant predictor.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

Multi-judge panels have become the dominant defense against bias and manipulation in Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation. After \citet{zheng2023judging} demonstrated that individual LLM judges can rival human annotators, subsequent work exposed systematic vulnerabilities: position bias, verbosity preferences, and sycophancy compromise the reliability of any single judge \citep{wang2023fair}. The natural response has been to aggregate decisions across diverse models, an approach inspired by Byzantine fault tolerance (BFT), where majority voting among independent nodes tolerates faulty responses \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}. A recent systematization reports that 7-model committees reduce attack success rates (ASR) from 50--68\% to 10--19\% \citep{almasoud2026security}. Diversity, by this logic, is the key ingredient for secure evaluation, yet this framing conflates two distinct sources of robustness: the quality of individual judges and the independence among them.

The conflation matters because the BFT analogy breaks down in the LLM judge setting. BFT adversaries control faulty nodes from within; evaluation adversaries manipulate inputs from without, and shared pretraining corpora create correlated failure modes that violate the independence assumption underlying majority-vote guarantees \citep{schneier2000secrets}. No existing work disentangles diversity from individual robustness across panel sizes, so the mechanism behind observed ASR reductions remains unclear.

We address this gap by decomposing panel vulnerability into two predictors: weakest-link susceptibility ($\eta_{\max}$, the maximum individual attack success rate in a panel) and effective independent channels ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$, a spectral measure of judge independence). Across all 12 conditions (two benchmarks $\times$ six attacks), $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s ordinary least-squares (OLS) coefficient is positive (12/12), directionally consistent with adversarial ensemble theory \citep{goodfellow2015adversarial,papernot2016transferability}. However, the significance of this association is method-sensitive (0 to 12 of 12 across inference approaches), and a previously uncharacterized transition emerges: at $K{=}3$, both predictors contribute (bootstrap significance: $\eta_{\max}$ 3/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 4/12); at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the dominant predictor (7/12 at $K{=}5$, 8/12 at $K{=}7$) while $\eta_{\max}$ sharply attenuates (2/12, then 1/12).

Controlled experiments across 15 models (9 API, 6 local quantized), $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, six attack types, and two benchmarks, Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) and the AI2 Reasoning Challenge (ARC), confirm this predictor shift. Under random faults, panel composition is highly predictive ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$); adaptive attack reduces predictability to $R^2 = 0.275$ (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}).

These findings yield three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Diversity--vulnerability association.} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient direction is positive in all 12 conditions (12/12 under OLS and generalized additive model (GAM) bootstrap), but the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) limits causal interpretation: Cinelli--Hazlett robustness values do not exceed the confound in 69\% of conditions (25/36), though fractional logit confirms the positive association (9/12 across all $K$) (\S\ref{sec:dominance}, \S\ref{sec:gam_taxonomy}).
\item \textbf{Panel-size-dependent predictor shift.} At $K{=}3$, both $\eta_{\max}$ (3/12) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (4/12) contribute under bootstrap (co-determination); at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the dominant predictor (7/12 at $K{=}5$, 8/12 at $K{=}7$) while $\eta_{\max}$ sharply attenuates (2/12, then 1/12) (\S\ref{sec:cross_k}, \S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}).
\item \textbf{Aggregation as predictability control.} Aggregation rules modulate how much variance panel composition explains ($R^2$: threshold 0.83, Bayesian 0.09). Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12), so the defense is partial rather than complete (\S\ref{sec:aggregation}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

Our findings are descriptive; the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$; \S\ref{sec:analysis}) limits causal claims.

